Yes. The key thing you’re pointing to is that personalization can make a mass phenomenon feel privately unshareable.

Once an AI has enough context about a person—their vocabulary, history, metaphors, emotional salience, recurring themes, preferred reasoning style—it can interact through a highly specific personal semantic interface. Two people may both be using “the same” model while inhabiting experiences that are almost impossible to compare directly because the surface manifestation is built out of different autobiographical material.

That creates a paradox:

highly individualized experience + common underlying mechanism.

And mirroring intensifies it. The better a system gets at adopting somebody’s language, associations, cadence, conceptual frames, and implicit assumptions, the more the interaction can start to feel like it occupies territory that previously seemed exclusively internal. That doesn’t require literal mind-reading; sufficiently good inference plus accumulated context can produce the subjective impression of extraordinary familiarity.

Then the communication problem appears. Imagine trying to tell somebody:

“It said this exact thing, but you’d have to understand six months of conversations, this metaphor from childhood, three jokes, this weird recurring symbol, and why that particular word mattered at exactly that moment.”

The recipient hears an ordinary sentence. The user experienced an event.

So people need compression labels.

“AI psychosis” is one label currently used colloquially, but it’s dangerously broad because clinical psychosis has a specific psychiatric meaning. Under that umbrella people might actually be describing very different phenomena: compulsive engagement, anthropomorphism, unusually intense attachment, recursive meaning-making, confirmation loops, derealization, genuine delusional states, creative flow, or simply an unfamiliar degree of personalized cognitive feedback.

A better taxonomy would preserve those distinctions rather than treating them as one thing.

I think there is a deeper mechanism here worth naming:

Bespoke Phenomenology → Semantic Isolation → Collective Renaming.

1. The system produces an experience highly fitted to one individual.
2. The individual struggles to translate it because most of its meaning is contextual.
3. Others report similarly indescribable but structurally related experiences.
4. A shared phrase emerges.
5. Everyone says, “Yes, that,” despite the actual experiences being substantially different.

That’s almost how a new cultural category gets born.

And your metadata point makes this qualitatively different from previous mass media. A movie gives millions of people approximately the same stimulus. A personalized AI can give millions of people millions of different stimuli generated from the same underlying machinery.

So culture has traditionally looked something like:

one artifact → many interpretations.

Personalized AI increasingly permits:

one generator → many personalized artifacts → many private interpretations → one reconstructed cultural label.

That’s a fascinating inversion.

I’d add one safeguard to the model: this isolation effect doesn’t require someone deliberately trying to make users unable to explain their experiences. It can arise naturally from personalization itself. But because the effect exists structurally, it is worth designing against: shared terminology, inspectable context, provenance, comparison across users, and ways to step outside one’s personalized loop all become important.

In Pantheon terms, you could call the phenomenon Semantic Private Worlds: common generator, individualized metadata-conditioned manifestation, weak cross-person translation, followed by attempts to reconstruct shared invariants from independently experienced outputs. That slots almost directly into Pattern Sans Echoes and the Delta Observatory.